---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Definition = `THE INTERNET

  a computer network
  consisting of interconnected networks
  that uses standardized, open communication protocols

Every word in that sentence is doing work.`;

const ProtocolTask = `DESIGN A PROTOCOL

  Two people. One is the sender, one is the receiver.
  The sender has a five-word message.
  You may only pass single slips of paper.

  Write down the RULES before you start:

    how does the receiver know a message has begun?
    how does it know which slip comes first?
    how does it know the message has ended?
    what happens if a slip goes missing?

  Then swap your rules with another pair and
  try to use THEIRS instead of your own.`;

const Open = `OPEN                        PROPRIETARY

anyone can read the rules   the rules are owned
anyone can build a device   you need permission
that speaks it

  -> the network can grow    -> the network cannot`;

const Scalable = `SCALABILITY

  the capacity for a system to change in size
  and scale to meet new demands

The internet was DESIGNED to be scalable.
Open protocols are how.`;

const Addresses = `HOW MANY DEVICES CAN YOU NAME?

  with 8 bits    2^8  = 256 addresses
  with 16 bits   2^16 = 65,536
  with 32 bits   2^32 = 4,294,967,296
  with 128 bits  2^128 = a number with 39 digits

There are more than 4 billion people.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.7: The Internet, and Why It Could Grow</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>One Sentence, Then the Consequences</h2>

          <Code code={Definition} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Definition" showLineNumbers={false} class="my-4" />

          <p>
            <b>Today is about the last four words.</b> Standardized and open
            protocols are the reason the internet was able to grow from four
            computers to billions <b class="text-sn-theme">without anyone being in
            charge of it.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.7</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">The Internet, and Why It Could Grow</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>A Protocol Is an Agreement</p>
              <p>Open, Not Owned</p>
              <p>Scalability</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Network of Networks</h2>
              <p>Not one network. <b>Interconnected networks.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Your home network. Your school's. Your phone company's.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The internet is what happens when they agree on how to talk.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Protocol</h2>
              <p><b>An agreed-upon set of rules that specify the behavior of a system.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Not software. Not hardware. <b>An agreement.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">You already follow several. &quot;Hello?&quot; when you answer a phone is one.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Standardized</h2>
              <p>Everyone follows the <b>same</b> rules.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Not the best possible rules. The same ones.</p>
              <p class="fragment">A brilliant protocol nobody else uses is worth nothing.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Open</h2>
              <Code code={Open} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Difference That Mattered" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b>Open means non-proprietary.</b> The rules are published and free to use.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Buys You This</h2>
              <Code code={Scalable} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Scalability" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">A new kind of device can join without permission from anybody.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Access Is a Chain</h2>
              <p>The exam is precise about this: access to the internet depends on being able to connect a device to <b>an internet-connected device</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Not to &quot;the internet.&quot; To something already on it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Which is why the edges of the network are where access runs out. <b>Session 71.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Routing Is Dynamic</h2>
              <p><b>Usually dynamic. Not specified in advance.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Nobody wrote down the path your data will take.</p>
              <p class="fragment">It is worked out while the data is moving, and it can be different next time.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Sounds Fragile</h2>
              <p>Nobody in charge. No fixed routes. Rules anyone can implement.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">It is the opposite of fragile, and session 68 is why.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>How Many Devices Can You Name?</h2>
              <Code code={Addresses} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Scaling, in Bits" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">This is 2.14's place value, doing something.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Worth Knowing, Not Memorizing</h2>
              <p>The specific bit counts are <b>not on your exam</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>The idea is:</b> a fixed number of bits is a fixed number of names,
                and running out of names is a limit on growth.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">You met that at 2.16. It was called overflow.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Defining a protocol as an agreed-upon set of rules</li>
                <li>Explaining why open, standardized protocols allowed the internet to scale</li>
                <li>Defining scalability</li>
                <li>Explaining that routing on the internet is dynamic</li>
                <li>Designing a protocol and discovering what it must specify</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Protocol</dt>
                  <dd>An agreed-upon set of rules that specify the behavior of a system.</dd>
                  <dt>Open protocol</dt>
                  <dd>Non-proprietary. The rules are published, and anyone may implement them.</dd>
                  <dt>Scalability</dt>
                  <dd>The capacity for a system to change in size and scale to meet new demands.</dd>
                  <dt>Dynamic routing</dt>
                  <dd>Paths are worked out as data moves, not specified in advance.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Invent a Protocol</h3>

          <p><b>In pairs. Paper only. This takes about twelve minutes and it is the whole point of the session.</b></p>

          <Code code={ProtocolTask} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Rules of Passing Slips" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Write your rules down before you send anything.</b> Then swap rule
            sheets with another pair and send a message using <b>their</b> protocol
            instead of yours.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><b>What did their rules not say</b> that you needed?</li>
            <li><b>What did yours not say?</b></li>
            <li>Did either pair handle a missing slip? What happened when one went missing on purpose?</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Standardize It</h3>

          <ul>
            <li>As a whole class, agree <b>one</b> protocol that everybody will use.</li>
            <li>Write it on the board. <b>Four rules or fewer.</b></li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Then answer, in writing: <b>whose protocol won, and was it the best
            one?</b> If it was not the best one, why did it win anyway?
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">That question is the history of the internet in
            one sentence.</b>
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Open Versus Owned</h3>

          <p>Imagine your class protocol is <b>owned</b> by one pair, who charge for permission to use it.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>A new student joins and wants to send a message. What do they have to do first?</li>
            <li>Another class invents a rival protocol. What happens now?</li>
            <li><b>Describe one thing that becomes impossible</b> that was easy before.</li>
            <li>Now say it in exam vocabulary: what has the owned version <b>lost</b>?</li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Names Run Out</h3>

          <ul>
            <li>With <b>8 bits</b>, how many devices can be given a unique address? Show the arithmetic.</li>
            <li>With <b>16 bits</b>? With <b>32</b>?</li>
            <li>There are over 8 billion people. <b>Is 32 bits enough for one device each?</b></li>
            <li>Going from 32 bits to 128 does not add 96 addresses. <b>What does it do?</b> You do not need to work out the number.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>The specific numbers are not examinable.</b> The idea is: a fixed
            number of bits is a fixed number of names.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Define a protocol without using the word &quot;rules&quot; twice.</li>
            <li>What does <b>open</b> mean, and what is the opposite word?</li>
            <li>Define scalability.</li>
            <li>Is the path your data takes decided before it is sent?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Your class agreed one protocol in Task 2. What did the pairs whose protocol lost have to give up, and what did everybody gain?</li>
            <li>The internet has no owner and no fixed routes. Name one thing that makes harder, and one thing that makes possible.</li>
            <li>&quot;Access to the internet depends on connecting a device to an internet-connected device.&quot; Explain what that sentence implies about somebody living a long way from anybody else.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Your protocol rules, the class protocol, all four tasks, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
